  },
  "body": "`Values.convertToString(schema, value)` accepted a schema but never used it when\nserializing `java.util.Date` values. `dateFormatFor(Date)` picked the format purely\nfrom the millisecond value, so any `Timestamp` falling exactly on midnight UTC\n(`getTime() % MILLIS_PER_DAY == 0`) was emitted as `yyyy-MM-dd`, silently dropping\nthe time component. Connectors producing timestamp headers (e.g. MongoDB source)\nhit this via `SimpleHeaderConverter`; on the way back, `parseString` then yielded a\n`Date` schema instead of `Timestamp`.\nThis change makes date formatting schema-aware:\n- Adds `Values.dateFormatFor(Schema, Date)`, which selects the ISO-8601 pattern from\nthe logical type name (`Time`, `Date`, `Timestamp`) and falls back to the existing\nmillisecond heuristic when the schema is null or not a logical type.\n- Threads the schema through `append`/`appendIterable` so dates nested in `Struct`\nfields, array elements and map keys/values are formatted according to their\nelement schema. Key/value schemas are only consulted when the schema type is\nactually `MAP`/`ARRAY`, since `ConnectSchema` throws otherwise.\n- `Cast` SMT passes the field schema into `castToString`, so casting a midnight\n`Timestamp` field to `STRING` keeps the time component. Schemaless casts are\nunchanged.\nThe existing `dateFormatFor(Date)` and the `append`/`appendIterable` overloads are\nretained and delegate with a null schema, so schemaless callers and subclasses see\nno change.\n\nBehaviour change: when a schema *is* present, it is now authoritative. A `Date`\nschema value carrying non-zero millis within the day is emitted as `yyyy-MM-dd`,\nand a `Time` schema value is emitted as `HH:mm:ss.SSS'Z'` regardless of magnitude.\nPreviously both would have been guessed from the millisecond value.\n\nReviewers: vinayak-gaikwad (github:vinayak-gaikwad)\n",
